WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.350
The dream of the pneumatic tube will never
die.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:08.700
They’re a few quirks from mainstream —
an oddity at bank drive thrus, a retro vision

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.019
of a smokeless and frictionless future.

00:00:11.019 --> 00:00:15.490
But the hope lives with inventions like the
Hyperloop that try to combine pneumatic tubes

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:19.600
and maglev for high speed transport,
The thing most people don’t know is how

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.160
long the dream has been alive — and that
they’ve been trying to move people for more

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.380
than 150 years.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.000
Pneumatic tubes are pretty simple.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.830
At their most basic, a sealed tube allows
a fan to make a vacuum.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.749
When a canister pops into place, a fan whirs
to life and creates a difference in air pressure

00:00:36.749 --> 00:00:39.780
that sucks the tube to its destination.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:45.269
As early as the 1850s, people adapted artificial
vacuums for powering the mail system, and

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:48.350
they sent lots of packages — including cats.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.420
Part II
The New York City underground pneumatic mail

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.949
system launched in 1897.

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.379
It carried the mail from one post office to
another.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:01:01.289
The first cannister had a Bible, a flag, and
a copy of the Constitution.

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:04.309
As an inside joke, the second carried an imitation
peach.

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:08.270
The third had a black cat (it survived).

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.600
Operators called themselves “Rocketeers”
and at its peak the system could send 200,000

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.950
letters an hour.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.860
It wasn’t close to the first.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.210
Pneumatic mail systems were surprisingly common.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:24.110
They’d been running in London since the
1850s, and were in Paris, Prague, Philadelphia,

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.360
and tons of other places.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:32.520
But as cities spread out and automobiles developed,
tubes became too difficult to install, maintain,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.610
and staff.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:38.710
They saw more widespread adoption in department
stores, offices, and yes, banks.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.300
NASA used tubes for memos.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.960
As interoffice mail, tubes were more practical,
until smaller offices and email made them

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.720
an extravagance there, too.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.710
But the dream was always bigger than that.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.600
They wanted to put people in the canisters.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.979
Part III
Before the mail was the pneumatic railway,

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:03.009
including New York’s first subway system,
30 years before construction of today’s

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:05.159
familiar underground subway began.

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:10.869
Opened in 1870, Alfred Ely Beach’s experimental
subway debuted under Broadway, modeled on

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:12.819
experiments in England.

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:20.260
It sold more than 400,000 short rides as just
an experiment: for New Yorkers of the era,

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.800
it wasn’t a subway —
It was an advance in civilization.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.879
Like London’s Crystal Palace atmospheric
roadway in the 1860s, it promised easy, clean,

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:28.879
transport.

00:02:28.879 --> 00:02:33.480
But Beach’s proof of concept closed before
more stations could be built.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.659
Investors got scared.

00:02:34.659 --> 00:02:38.459
There was no pneumatic disaster, just reality.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:42.420
The same thing that eventually took down the
mail took down the pneumatic subway.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:45.940
It wasn’t easy or cheap enough to be sustainable.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:50.200
The airless tube had too much friction.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.770
Pneumatic tubes are still around.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.380
Until 2011, they were used in at least one
McDonald’s drive-thru.

00:02:56.380 --> 00:02:59.549
And they’re still used in hospitals to transport
lab samples

00:02:59.549 --> 00:03:02.659
quickly and safely from
one floor to another.

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:06.540
But it’s the Hyperloop that has the original
spirit, the conviction that the pneumatic

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.250
tube isn’t a pipe dream.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:13.580
It’s the idea part Victorian, part Jetsons:
that tubes won’t just push people from one

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:19.220
place to another,
But maybe into the future, too.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.290
Any history of pneumatic tubes is going to
involve a lot of omissions.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:29.260
I had to leave out the trash disposal on Roosevelt
Island, pneumatic dating, and also the pioneering

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:36.379
pneumatic mail in Philadelphia, where they
sent a rooster, guinea pigs, and a goldfish

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:40.819
in a goldfish bowl through the pneumatic — and
none of them were injured.

